"A schema compatibility graph shows which reader and writer versions can exchange data under a named compatibility rule.\n\nUse when: Plan independent producer and consumer upgrades. Explain whether a schema change preserves supported readers.\n\nAvoid when: Only structural field nesting needs explanation.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes identify schema versions or reader and writer versions. Directed edges state a precise can-read or can-write compatibility relation. Change annotations describe added, removed, renamed, or type-changed fields.\n\nBuild: Declare the serialization format and compatibility direction. List the supported writer and reader versions. Mark compatibility from actual checks or clearly labeled design analysis. Lay out versions chronologically while routing backward-read edges through separate lanes.\n\nCheck: Backward and forward compatibility use the named platform’s definitions. Transitive compatibility is not assumed from adjacent-version checks. Defaults and optionality are evaluated under the actual serialization rules. Version labels and compatibility arrows remain visually distinct.\n\nPitfalls: Calling a change safe merely because it adds a field. Confusing deployment sequence with proven data compatibility.\n\nExample: Reader v2 accepts Writer v1 records through a documented default, while Reader v1 cannot interpret a new required representation.\n\nAlternatives: Document schema diagram, Artifact promotion diagram."
